WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.315
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.615 --> 00:00:08.758
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.058 --> 00:00:18.641
Unit forty-four: Genius and Creativity. In this unit, you will discuss the nature of genius using cohesive, well-referenced speech.

4
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:20.512
genius.

5
00:00:21.212 --> 00:00:26.636
genius means exceptional intellectual or creative power, or a person who has it.

6
00:00:26.936 --> 00:00:30.503
Mozart is often called a musical genius.

7
00:00:31.203 --> 00:00:33.670
Your turn. Say: genius.

8
00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:37.173
genius.

9
00:00:38.183 --> 00:00:39.231
prodigy.

10
00:00:39.931 --> 00:00:43.091
prodigy means a young person with extraordinary talent.

11
00:00:43.391 --> 00:00:46.471
The chess prodigy beat adults at the age of eight.

12
00:00:47.171 --> 00:00:50.067
Your turn. Say: prodigy.

13
00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:53.571
prodigy.

14
00:00:54.583 --> 00:00:56.037
creative breakthrough.

15
00:00:56.737 --> 00:01:01.837
creative breakthrough means a sudden important advance in creative or scientific work.

16
00:01:02.137 --> 00:01:05.146
Her creative breakthrough came after years of failure.

17
00:01:05.846 --> 00:01:09.056
Your turn. Say: creative breakthrough.

18
00:01:11.756 --> 00:01:13.152
creative breakthrough.

19
00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:15.883
eureka moment.

20
00:01:16.583 --> 00:01:21.659
eureka moment means a sudden moment of discovery or insight.

21
00:01:21.959 --> 00:01:24.806
Archimedes had his eureka moment in the bath.

22
00:01:25.506 --> 00:01:29.273
Your turn. Say: eureka moment.

23
00:01:31.973 --> 00:01:33.821
eureka moment.

24
00:01:34.817 --> 00:01:36.120
divergent mind.

25
00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:41.339
divergent mind means a way of thinking that produces many different ideas.

26
00:01:41.639 --> 00:01:44.776
A divergent mind sees ten uses for a paperclip.

27
00:01:45.476 --> 00:01:48.326
Your turn. Say: divergent mind.

28
00:01:51.026 --> 00:01:52.340
divergent mind.

29
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.618
creative tension.

30
00:01:55.318 --> 00:01:59.791
creative tension means productive conflict that pushes people to new ideas.

31
00:02:00.091 --> 00:02:03.994
The creative tension between the two designers produced brilliant work.

32
00:02:04.694 --> 00:02:08.008
Your turn. Say: creative tension.

33
00:02:10.708 --> 00:02:11.965
creative tension.

34
00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:13.835
muse.

35
00:02:14.535 --> 00:02:18.055
muse means a person or force that inspires an artist.

36
00:02:18.355 --> 00:02:20.912
The Mekong was the poet's lifelong muse.

37
00:02:21.612 --> 00:02:25.159
Your turn. Say: muse.

38
00:02:27.859 --> 00:02:28.755
muse.

39
00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.146
inspiration myth.

40
00:02:31.846 --> 00:02:37.561
inspiration myth means the false belief that great work comes from sudden inspiration rather than effort.

41
00:02:37.861 --> 00:02:42.600
The inspiration myth hides the hard work behind every masterpiece.

42
00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.486
Your turn. Say: inspiration myth.

43
00:02:49.186 --> 00:02:50.489
inspiration myth.

44
00:02:51.483 --> 00:02:53.750
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:57.234
Ms. Sarah, are geniuses born or made?

46
00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:01.560
Both, though the latter is too often forgotten.

47
00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.966
What about prodigies like Mozart?

48
00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:13.082
He was talented, certainly. Yet he also practised for thousands of hours as a child; this is rarely mentioned.

49
00:03:14.383 --> 00:03:17.895
And eureka moments? Archimedes had one.

50
00:03:19.183 --> 00:03:24.645
He did, but only after years of thinking about the problem. Such moments reward preparation.

51
00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.147
So the inspiration myth is misleading?

52
00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:36.305
Precisely. That is why I tell students: talent opens the door; effort walks through it.

53
00:03:37.617 --> 00:03:41.172
Grammar focus: Cohesion and reference (extended).

54
00:03:41.472 --> 00:03:43.402
Reference words create cohesion.

55
00:03:43.702 --> 00:03:48.290
Geniuses are both born and made, though the latter is too often forgotten.

56
00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.578
Such moments reward preparation. That is why effort matters.

57
00:03:52.878 --> 00:03:59.452
Use 'the former/the latter', 'this', 'such' and substitution to connect ideas without repeating nouns.

58
00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:04.159
Now read and listen. The Myth of the Lonely Genius.

59
00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:09.628
Popular culture loves the image of the lonely genius struck by sudden inspiration.

60
00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:14.762
This picture, however, is largely an inspiration myth.

61
00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:23.379
Research suggests that even a prodigy needs years of deliberate practice before producing original work.

62
00:04:24.683 --> 00:04:32.383
The famous eureka moment usually follows long periods of preparation; such insights rarely come from nowhere.

63
00:04:33.683 --> 00:04:40.664
Many creative breakthroughs also emerge from creative tension within teams rather than from isolated individuals.

64
00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:48.292
Psychologists link creativity to a divergent mind, the ability to generate many possible ideas.

65
00:04:49.583 --> 00:04:55.275
An artist's muse may provide inspiration, but the latter is useless without discipline.

66
00:04:56.583 --> 00:05:02.298
Genius, then, may be best understood as rare talent multiplied by extraordinary effort.

67
00:05:03.583 --> 00:05:06.305
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:06.605 --> 00:05:09.743
A young person with extraordinary talent is a blank.

69
00:05:10.043 --> 00:05:15.577
A, prodigy. B, muse. C, eureka moment.

70
00:05:19.583 --> 00:05:21.687
The answer is A: prodigy.

71
00:05:23.183 --> 00:05:27.693
In 'tea or coffee? I prefer the latter', the speaker prefers blank.

72
00:05:27.993 --> 00:05:31.844
A, coffee. B, tea. C, neither.

73
00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:37.838
The answer is A: coffee.

74
00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:42.920
'Such moments reward preparation.' What does 'such' do?

75
00:05:43.220 --> 00:05:51.738
A, refers back to moments already mentioned. B, introduces a new idea. C, shows contrast.

76
00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:59.874
The answer is A: refers back to moments already mentioned.

77
00:06:01.383 --> 00:06:06.436
The false belief that great work comes only from sudden inspiration is the blank.

78
00:06:06.736 --> 00:06:12.723
A, inspiration myth. B, creative tension. C, divergent mind.

79
00:06:16.717 --> 00:06:19.099
The answer is A: inspiration myth.

80
00:06:20.583 --> 00:06:24.777
According to the text, where do many creative breakthroughs emerge from?

81
00:06:25.077 --> 00:06:31.761
A, creative tension within teams. B, isolated individuals only. C, luck.

82
00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:39.294
The answer is A: creative tension within teams.

83
00:06:40.783 --> 00:06:47.580
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is genius mainly a gift or the result of hard work?

84
00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:57.910
Part two: Use 'the former/the latter' and one summarising 'this' or 'such'.

85
00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:16.176
Talent and effort both matter, but the latter is more important. Mozart practised for thousands of hours, and this is often forgotten. Such stories show that genius is built, not just given.

86
00:07:16.476 --> 00:07:19.254
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:25.317
Great work today! You learned eight new words and cohesion and reference (extended).

88
00:07:25.617 --> 00:07:29.825
Today's value is creativity. Use your imagination when you play.

89
00:07:30.125 --> 00:07:33.544
See you in Unit forty-five. Goodbye!

90
00:07:33.844 --> 00:07:36.171
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

